WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:02.740
For this next section, we're going

2
00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:07.280
to dive very deep into our cameras, in particular,

3
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.019
talking about the difference between our game camera

4
00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:11.699
and our cinema cameras.

5
00:00:11.699 --> 00:00:14.419
So a game camera is traditionally

6
00:00:14.419 --> 00:00:17.339
what the player uses to view the world,

7
00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:18.539
to play through the world.

8
00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:21.780
When you play a game, you are possessing that character,

9
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.980
or if you're doing a third person or top down,

10
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.420
that camera is the camera into the world

11
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.100
and what the player sees through.

12
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.259
So traditionally, we use these game camera actors.

13
00:00:33.259 --> 00:00:35.340
In fact, if I go back to the editor,

14
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.259
I'll kind of work over in this section here,

15
00:00:37.259 --> 00:00:40.100
I'm going to go up to my windows, Place Actors,

16
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.549
and I'm just going to simply search for camera.

17
00:00:43.549 --> 00:00:45.630
And you see here is our camera actor.

18
00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.070
And I'll move this up so you see there's our camera actor.

19
00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:53.039
Move this up and it does, in fact, look blue.

20
00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:55.759
So taking just a quick look over in our Details panel

21
00:00:55.759 --> 00:00:57.520
here, you'll see that we have our camera.

22
00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.920
And for the camera settings, it's very basic.

23
00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.439
We have a projection mode.

24
00:01:02.439 --> 00:01:04.760
So go perspective or orthographic.

25
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.959
Our field of view, which you should be familiar with.

26
00:01:06.959 --> 00:01:09.400
Obviously, if it's smaller, it's a tighter field of view.

27
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.140
And if we go up, it's much wider.

28
00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.579
And we have our aspect ratio.

29
00:01:13.579 --> 00:01:15.000
As you can see, as I change this,

30
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.859
I can make it wider or skinnier.

31
00:01:17.859 --> 00:01:20.939
Now beyond this, we have a few camera options

32
00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:23.260
and we have the ability to do some post-processing.

33
00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.579
Now we're not going to cover post-processing in this class.

34
00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:28.019
But the important thing to note here

35
00:01:28.019 --> 00:01:31.739
is that we don't really have a lot of traditional cinema

36
00:01:31.739 --> 00:01:33.620
controls that we'll see here in just a second

37
00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.359
with our cinema camera actor.

38
00:01:35.359 --> 00:01:36.700
And that is by design.

39
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.019
This camera is meant to be a very basic camera because,

40
00:01:40.019 --> 00:01:42.060
again, in traditional games, we're not really

41
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.099
looking to overload a lot of post-processing effects

42
00:01:45.099 --> 00:01:47.560
or special different on-screen visuals.

43
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.939
We simply just want a camera that's looking into our world

44
00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:52.700
that the player can play through.

45
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.709
Now going to take a note real quick,

46
00:01:54.709 --> 00:01:56.709
we have this constraint aspect ratio.

47
00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.290
We'll talk about this in a little bit,

48
00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:00.709
but just know that it's within our Details panel.

49
00:02:00.709 --> 00:02:03.010
All right, so this is going to be just our regular game

50
00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:03.629
camera.

51
00:02:03.629 --> 00:02:05.030
Go ahead and press Escape.

52
00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.629
And I'm going to drag in a cinema camera actor.

53
00:02:07.629 --> 00:02:10.240
Let's go ahead and pull this up.

54
00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.280
And you notice that the visuals are different.

55
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.699
Here we have our gray camera posed as a blue.

56
00:02:15.699 --> 00:02:17.439
And if we go over to our Details panel,

57
00:02:17.439 --> 00:02:18.900
you'll see that we actually start

58
00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.240
to have a lot more control, which all of these controls

59
00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.560
are intrinsically tied to real world counterparts

60
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.800
to traditional cameras and cinematography.

61
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.539
So for example, we have our film back.

62
00:02:31.539 --> 00:02:33.039
I'll expand these out.

63
00:02:33.039 --> 00:02:35.319
And you see we can start playing with sensor widths.

64
00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:37.199
There are some presets here, but know

65
00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:39.759
that you can always override these to do custom.

66
00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:41.259
But these are just some presets

67
00:02:41.259 --> 00:02:43.680
based off of, again, traditional film,

68
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.580
traditional cameras, and the way they operate.

69
00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.780
Our lens setting, you'll see that we actually now started

70
00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.139
to talk about f-stops.

71
00:02:50.139 --> 00:02:52.300
We're talking about focal lengths.

72
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.020
This is important to understand, too,

73
00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:57.060
that if you've ever looked at that rich cinema feel,

74
00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.860
we've got this beautiful depth of field.

75
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.939
In fact, let me take the camera, and we'll move it

76
00:03:00.939 --> 00:03:01.580
down.

77
00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.250
And you'll see we're starting to get some of that.

78
00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.930
In fact, let's go ahead and just focus in on this little

79
00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.330
bush up here.

80
00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.770
So the bush is slightly fuzzy.

81
00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.009
And then it starts to get a little bit more in focus.

82
00:03:13.009 --> 00:03:17.139
And right now, in fact, if I turn on,

83
00:03:17.139 --> 00:03:19.580
I'm going to go back to our Focus Settings here, turn on

84
00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.580
our Draw Debug Focus Plane.

85
00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.900
And as I drag this in, we should

86
00:03:23.939 --> 00:03:25.819
see it come into focus.

87
00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:27.900
So this is going to be where the camera is focusing.

88
00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.740
So if I zoom in to put it right in the middle of this

89
00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.259
little plant here and uncheck it, you'll see now the

90
00:03:35.259 --> 00:03:38.860
actual plant is in focus, and the background is blurred.

91
00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.780
So it feels very cinematic, feels like a real camera.

92
00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.780
And that is by design.

93
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.900
So without going through all of these various different

94
00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.560
settings within the Cinema Camera, it's important to note,

95
00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.860
again, from a very just high-level, simplistic

96
00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.300
explanation of this, is that our blue camera is going to be

97
00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:59.180
a very simple camera, and then our gray camera is going to

98
00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:00.580
be our very cinematic.

99
00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.060
So hopefully that makes sense.

100
00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:06.259
So inside of our sequence, traditionally speaking,

101
00:04:06.259 --> 00:04:08.500
we're going to want to use this Cinema Camera

102
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.139
because, again, we're going to want to take advantage

103
00:04:10.139 --> 00:04:12.340
of all of those really high details.

104
00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.659
Now, you could use a player camera.

105
00:04:14.659 --> 00:04:16.100
That's completely fine.

106
00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.459
But for the Cinema Camera actor, it's going to allow us

107
00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:20.379
to do, again, those depth of field.

108
00:04:20.379 --> 00:04:22.259
It's going to allow us to do focus tracking,

109
00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.779
a lot more controls that, typically speaking,

110
00:04:24.779 --> 00:04:26.600
we're going to want to have

111
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:29.180
when building out our sequences.

112
00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.379
So a couple things to note, as I had mentioned,

113
00:04:31.379 --> 00:04:33.399
one of the important things is this

114
00:04:33.399 --> 00:04:36.660
constrain aspect ratio.

115
00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.699
So it's important to note, I don't know if you've played

116
00:04:38.699 --> 00:04:40.980
a game before, and you're playing your game,

117
00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.639
and let's say that you have an ultra-wide,

118
00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:45.220
you have a much wider field of view.

119
00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.860
And if a game has been made to where you go

120
00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:50.139
into that sequence and you have these black bars

121
00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:53.660
on the side, that's probably because the sequence itself,

122
00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.740
which we can specify the ratio within our camera,

123
00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:59.540
was probably set up as a 16 by nine.

124
00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:02.920
So if you use your camera and you uncheck

125
00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:05.779
constrain aspect ratio, for users that have

126
00:05:05.779 --> 00:05:09.259
a non-16 by nine ratio monitor,

127
00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:10.639
or whatever they're viewing it on,

128
00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:13.399
it'll actually allow it to stretch to the full length.

129
00:05:13.399 --> 00:05:15.000
So just keep that in mind in your back pocket

130
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:15.980
as you're building these.

131
00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:17.759
It's a very simple button that we can,

132
00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:20.040
or toggle that we can just check on or off

133
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.759
to be able to get rid of those black bars on the side.

134
00:05:22.759 --> 00:05:24.139
We talked about our film back.

135
00:05:24.139 --> 00:05:25.959
So again, this is gonna be our sensor,

136
00:05:25.959 --> 00:05:26.800
our width and height.

137
00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:28.959
We talked about various different lens settings,

138
00:05:28.959 --> 00:05:30.379
our focus settings.

139
00:05:30.379 --> 00:05:33.360
Now, if I jump over to our sequence real quick,

140
00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.259
and I'm gonna go ahead and stretch out our timeline here.

141
00:05:38.259 --> 00:05:40.519
You'll see that we have a couple different shots.

142
00:05:40.519 --> 00:05:42.800
So we have this one where it's following,

143
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:44.860
and then we have our second shot,

144
00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.720
which you'll notice is actually tracking with the player.

145
00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:50.720
And that's because we've enabled look at tracking

146
00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.560
with our camera to track this particular character

147
00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.199
as you move across the screen.

148
00:05:56.199 --> 00:05:57.160
And then here, you'll see,

149
00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:59.639
we also have some various different lens settings.

150
00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:02.800
Our focus is directly on this NPC,

151
00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:03.959
for lack of better terms.

152
00:06:03.959 --> 00:06:05.720
And you'll see this blurred in the background.

153
00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:08.360
And again, that's something that we can take advantage of

154
00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.600
by using a cinema camera,

155
00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:14.189
as opposed to just a regular camera.

156
00:06:14.189 --> 00:06:16.389
And then finally, we talked briefly that

157
00:06:16.389 --> 00:06:18.110
within the cameras themselves,

158
00:06:18.589 --> 00:06:20.509
we do have post-processing effects.

159
00:06:20.509 --> 00:06:22.990
Now, something just to throw in your back pocket

160
00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:24.550
to keep in mind is,

161
00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.750
I don't typically recommend

162
00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:29.509
using post-processing on the camera,

163
00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:34.269
but rather using a post-processing volume with that.

164
00:06:34.269 --> 00:06:36.069
The advantage that you have there is,

165
00:06:36.069 --> 00:06:37.769
say for example, that you set up a camera

166
00:06:37.769 --> 00:06:41.990
with a specific lens ratio, a specific F-stop, right?

167
00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:45.389
Like a camera that you want to use quite often

168
00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:48.509
throughout an entire sequence or multiple sequences.

169
00:06:48.509 --> 00:06:50.550
By decoupling your post-processing volume,

170
00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:53.670
it means that you can swap in different cameras

171
00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:56.149
or you can change things out as needed.

172
00:06:56.149 --> 00:06:57.589
And you're not locked to just having

173
00:06:57.589 --> 00:07:01.350
those post-processing settings on that one specific camera.

174
00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:02.230
So just keep that in mind

175
00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:05.029
that you can still do all the same post-processing effects

176
00:07:05.029 --> 00:07:07.069
through a post-processing volume,

177
00:07:07.069 --> 00:07:08.350
but as a handy feature,

178
00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:10.350
we have some of those post-processing things

179
00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:11.810
directly on the camera itself.

180
00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:13.189
So just keep that in mind.

181
00:07:13.189 --> 00:07:14.810
The other thing to keep in mind here is that,

182
00:07:15.089 --> 00:07:17.009
as we're building out our sequence,

183
00:07:17.009 --> 00:07:19.449
we can have multiple cameras.

184
00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:22.110
In fact, if we jump back over to our sequence,

185
00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:24.170
you'll see here actually we have our three cameras.

186
00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:27.189
So CCA1, O2, and O3.

187
00:07:27.189 --> 00:07:29.250
In fact, these exist in our world.

188
00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:30.730
So let me go ahead and just delete

189
00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:33.439
the example cameras we have here.

190
00:07:33.439 --> 00:07:36.199
So I'll select my CCA1, press F to frame,

191
00:07:36.199 --> 00:07:39.050
and you'll see there is that camera.

192
00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:41.850
CCAO2 is down here.

193
00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:44.089
So you see that we can have multiple cameras

194
00:07:44.089 --> 00:07:46.480
within our scene.

195
00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:48.480
Now within each of these shots,

196
00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.879
we can actually associate those with a particular camera.

197
00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:52.939
So if I right click and go to properties,

198
00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:55.920
you'll see here, camera binding ID.

199
00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.560
Now fun fact, pro tip here,

200
00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:58.920
if you've ever opened up a sequence

201
00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:00.800
and you get this red bar around there saying

202
00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:03.199
the camera binding has been de-associated,

203
00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:04.720
but you're looking at your scene and you're like,

204
00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:07.639
but wait, my camera's in the scene, what's happened?

205
00:08:07.639 --> 00:08:09.439
It happens occasionally.

206
00:08:09.439 --> 00:08:11.800
All you need to do is go back into the shot itself

207
00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:15.199
and rebind your camera and all the keys

208
00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:18.839
should still be there and it should work like normal.

209
00:08:18.839 --> 00:08:22.060
So just a couple other cameras to keep in mind.

210
00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:25.079
We do have the scene capture component 2D,

211
00:08:25.079 --> 00:08:27.800
which is a special type of camera that allows us

212
00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:30.720
to essentially capture our scene

213
00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:35.139
and render what is being fed into that camera to a texture.

214
00:08:35.139 --> 00:08:37.159
In fact, this is one of the cameras that used

215
00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:40.100
if you're trying to capture like HDRI cubes

216
00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.100
within your editor.

217
00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.539
Yes, that is the thing that you can do, it is awesome.

218
00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:46.580
And you use this camera essentially to be the eyes

219
00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:49.820
in the world, the position, and then you will capture

220
00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:53.500
whatever it's seen and render it to a texture.

221
00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:56.139
We also have a cinema capture component 2D.

222
00:08:56.139 --> 00:08:58.220
Now this is new as a caveat,

223
00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.460
it is in beta mode as of 5.3.

224
00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:01.980
So just kind of keep this in mind,

225
00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.600
but it essentially just extends the capabilities

226
00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:09.299
of the capture component to allow for a few other

227
00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:12.340
nice visual effects such as our post-processing,

228
00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:14.679
OCIO and lens distortion.

229
00:09:14.679 --> 00:09:17.019
And then we also have a VCam actor.

230
00:09:17.019 --> 00:09:19.080
Now this is outside of essentially what we're doing

231
00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:21.220
for this particular class,

232
00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:23.379
but as I had mentioned in our plugins,

233
00:09:23.379 --> 00:09:24.980
there are different things that we can use.

234
00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:28.460
So say for example, we can use the Apple AR kit

235
00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:31.500
to capture your face via motion capture.

236
00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:34.259
We can use motion capture suits as well.

237
00:09:34.259 --> 00:09:36.600
And one of the other really cool features is

238
00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:41.600
by leveraging an Apple device such as a iPad or an iPhone,

239
00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.240
we can actually transmit the location,

240
00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:48.460
rotation, position information of that iOS device.

241
00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:51.379
Also works for Android, but iOS is more supported.

242
00:09:51.379 --> 00:09:54.639
We can translate that information directly in engine

243
00:09:54.639 --> 00:09:57.759
to a camera actor, in this case using a VCam actor

244
00:09:57.759 --> 00:09:59.360
through the Live Link plugin.

245
00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:01.279
This is an amazing tool to use.

246
00:10:01.279 --> 00:10:04.279
So if you are familiar with traditional film,

247
00:10:04.279 --> 00:10:07.279
one of the things that makes something feel more natural

248
00:10:07.279 --> 00:10:10.600
as opposed to synthetic is the camera movement, right?

249
00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:13.399
Traditionally, when a film is working, right?

250
00:10:13.399 --> 00:10:15.720
It's either on a operator's shoulder

251
00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.000
or it's on a dollier track.

252
00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.519
And there's these very subtle movements that you get,

253
00:10:20.519 --> 00:10:23.720
whether it's from the mechanicals of the camera itself

254
00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:26.679
moving along the track or the operator's slight movements

255
00:10:26.679 --> 00:10:28.360
that we've grown accustomed to

256
00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.840
as we've watched film over the years.

257
00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:32.919
All that to say, so you can use a VCam actor

258
00:10:32.919 --> 00:10:35.200
coupled with, say for example, an iPad,

259
00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:38.960
and actually record all of that information

